/// The public control for the visibility gate.
///
/// This module used to also carry a `private secret()` that another module
/// called successfully -- the defect this project was built to pin. The gate
/// landed on 2026-08-05, so that case moved to
/// tests/tests/projects/visibility_gate, where it is a compile_fail asserting
/// E0353 through each of the three binding doors.
///
/// What stays here is the half that must keep WORKING and must keep RUNNING:
/// a public function called from another module, checked for its value. A
/// compile_fail project can only assert that a diagnostic is ABSENT, which
/// would still hold if public cross-module calls had broken for some
/// unrelated reason.
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionTripwire::Vault;

public function door() -> i32 { return 1; }

/// KNOWN DEFECT, and the reason §7.1's rider survives the 2026-08-10 gates.
///
/// The E0503 gate judges a type where the source NAMES one -- annotations and
/// struct literals. A value whose type is never written reaches no gate, so
/// this `private` type crosses a module boundary through `issue`'s inferred
/// return type. Spelled `private` deliberately: the default is public.
private type struct Ledger {
    v: i32;

    get(&this) -> i32 { return this.v; }
}

/// The escape hatch: the caller writes nothing that mentions `Ledger`.
/// Its own return annotation is same-module, so it is legal here.
public function issue() -> Ledger { return Ledger { v: 7 }; }
